# ND-120 - local settings. You normally do NOT copy this file by hand:
#
#     python3 configure.py            (Windows: py configure.py)
#
# finds the tools, asks for what it cannot find, and writes local.mk next to
# this file. local.mk is machine-local and gitignored; nothing in it is ever
# committed. This example lists every name configure.py knows, so a person
# setting up by hand (or reading what a value means) has one place to look.
#
# Syntax: plain "NAME := value" lines - no make functions or $(...) in a
# value. In make syntax a '$' is written '$$' and a '#' is written '\#'.
#
# Who reads local.mk:
#   * every board Makefile, the Verilog/ Makefiles that need a setting, and
#     the sim/test Makefiles, through paths.mk at the repository root;
#   * the PowerShell scripts, through Verilog/fpga/paths.ps1;
#   * the Vivado and Gowin Tcl scripts, through Verilog/fpga/paths.tcl;
#   * the Python and shell helpers, through configure.py (configure.setting).
#
# One rule everywhere: a value set in the ENVIRONMENT wins over local.mk.
# Under make, a value on the command line (make NAME=value) wins over both.
#
# A target checks the settings IT needs before doing any work, and stops with
# one message naming the variable and the command that fixes it. See
# CONTRIBUTING.md "Local settings" for the table of names.
#
# Path forms: a Windows program (vivado.bat, gw_sh.exe) is always written as a
# Windows path, even when configure.py ran in WSL, because it is started
# through powershell.exe. Folders are written the way the shell that ran
# configure.py sees them; every reader converts X:\... <-> /mnt/x/... itself.

# Where builds go. REQUIRED for every board build: each board writes
# everything - bitstream, reports, timing-analysis runs, Vivado's .Xil,
# journal and log - to <this folder>/<board>/. A Windows program started from
# WSL needs it on a Windows drive (/mnt/<drive>/...).
# ND120_BUILD_DIR := <folder>

# The Vivado program: vivado.bat on Windows, vivado on Linux. Needed by the
# Basys3, Cmod A7, Nexys 4 DDR, QMTECH and MEGA65 builds. make VIVADO=<program>
# overrides it for one run.
# ND120_VIVADO := <Vivado install>\bin\vivado.bat

# Vivado licence file list, handed to Vivado as XILINXD_LICENSE_FILE when that
# is not already set. Several files are separated by ';'. Unset = the Windows
# user (then machine) XILINXD_LICENSE_FILE.
# ND120_VIVADO_LICENSE := <licence file>;<another licence file>

# Gowin EDA gw_sh (Tang Nano 20K: make gowin, gowin_build.ps1).
# ND120_GOWIN := <Gowin install>\IDE\bin\gw_sh.exe

# Quartus bin folder. Nothing reads it today (the MiSTer build runs Quartus in
# Docker); the name is fixed so a future script uses this one.
# ND120_QUARTUS := <Quartus install>\bin64

# oss-cad-suite folder - the install the shell you build from uses. The Tang
# Makefile (OSS flow) puts its bin/ on PATH; Verilog/ND-120-Yosys/synh.bat
# calls its environment.bat; the BUS-IF gate Windows example names it.
# ND120_OSS_CAD_SUITE := <oss-cad-suite folder>

# w64devkit folder (only for the BUS-IF gate Windows example).
# ND120_W64DEVKIT := <w64devkit folder>

# Folder holding the other ND repositories side by side (ND110Compile,
# nd120uc, NDDeviceCore, RetroTerm, ...). Optional: the instruction-verify
# golden traces and Verilog/sim/compare_boot.py need it.
# ND_REPOS := <folder holding the ND repositories>

# An ILA capture exported from Vivado as CSV (Verilog/sim/analyze_ila.py,
# Verilog/sim/compare_boot.py).
# ND120_ILA_CSV := <file>

# Where long trace captures are kept (too big to commit).
# ND120_ORACLE_DIR := <folder>

# The Verilog/sim folder as WSL sees it (Verilog/sim/nd120_probe.py). Optional:
# by default it is worked out from the script's own location.
# ND120_SIM_WSLDIR := <folder>

# An EMPTY folder for `make fresh-build` (any board folder): the current
# commit is cloned into it, configured with a build folder inside the clone,
# and built there - to prove the build needs nothing outside the repository.
# Usually given on the command line: make fresh-build ND120_FRESH_DIR=<folder>
# ND120_FRESH_DIR := <empty folder>
